Hua.Zhu.1","B.Fogelberg.10","Shan.Gui.Zhou.1","F.S.Stephens.15","L.Hildingsson.5","J.Molnar.3","Xiao.Guang.Wu.1","Bao.Guo.Dong.1","J.E.Draper.1","J.Mierzejewski.1","N.Marginean.2","D.Curien.18","C.t.Li.1","J.N.Scheurer.1","S.Zhu.6","D.Bazzacco.1","A.A.Pasternak.2","K.O.Zell.2","X.G.Wu.7","J.Srebrny.1","J.Gizon.4","C.Schuck.3","L.Kaubler.1","Shao.Qiang.Zhang.1","L.E.Samuelson.4","K.E.Zyromski.1","Guang.Sheng.Li.1","P.Papka.1","Yan.Wen.Liu.1","S.Gros.2","C.J.Chiara.1","P.Joshi.3"],"legacy_version":"20170216152502.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Other"},{"term":"Experiment-Nucl"}],"first_author":{"full_name":"Lieder, E. O.","last_name":"Lieder","first_name":"E. O.","recid":2318178},"control_number":1331945,"dois":[{"source":"APS","value":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.112.202502"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Lieder:2014ria"],"abstracts":[{"source":"APS","value":"<p>The nature of the chiral candidate bands in <inline-formula><mml:math display=\"inline\"><mml:mrow><mml:mmultiscripts><mml:mrow><mml:mi>Ag</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mprescripts/><mml:none/><mml:mrow><mml:mn>106</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:mmultiscripts></mml:mrow></mml:math></inline-formula>, one of only two known examples of candidates which actually cross, is investigated experimentally and theoretically. Lifetimes have been determined for these bands in <inline-formula><mml:math display=\"inline\"><mml:mrow><mml:mmultiscripts><mml:mrow><mml:mi>Ag</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mprescripts/><mml:none/><mml:mrow><mml:mn>106</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:mmultiscripts></mml:mrow></mml:math></inline-formula> using the Doppler-shift attenuation method with the <inline-formula><mml:math display=\"inline\"><mml:mi>γ</mml:mi></mml:math></inline-formula>-detector array AFRODITE. The level scheme of <inline-formula><mml:math display=\"inline\"><mml:mrow><mml:mmultiscripts><mml:mrow><mml:mi>Ag</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mprescripts/><mml:none/><mml:mrow><mml:mn>106</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:mmultiscripts></mml:mrow></mml:math></inline-formula> has been extended, and three negative-parity bands have been observed to high spins. Configurations were assigned to the negative-parity bands based on a quasiparticle alignment analysis and on configuration-fixed constrained relativistic mean field calculations. The excitation energies, <inline-formula><mml:math display=\"inline\"><mml:mi>B</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>M</mml:mi><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mml:mo></mml:math></inline-formula> and <inline-formula><mml:math display=\"inline\"><mml:mi>B</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>E</mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mml:mo></mml:math></inline-formula> values, as well as <inline-formula><mml:math display=\"inline\"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>B</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>M</mml:mi><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>/</mml:mo><mml:mi>B</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>E</mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></inline-formula> ratios have been compared with results of particle-rotor model calculations. From the investigations, it is concluded that the three close-lying negative-parity bands are a two-quasiparticle high-<inline-formula><mml:math display=\"inline\"><mml:mi>K</mml:mi></mml:math></inline-formula> band and a pair of four-quasiparticle bands. The proposal that the two lowest-lying bands are chiral partners has not been confirmed.</p>","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"APS"}}],"titles":[{"title":"Resolution of Chiral Conundrum in Ag106 : Doppler-Shift Lifetime Investigation"}],"imprints":[{"date":"2014-05-20"}],"curated":false},"created":"2014-12-02T00:00:00+00:00","id":"1331945","updated":"2024-07-26T00:21:53.759412+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1331945?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1331945?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1331945?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1331945?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1331945?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1331945?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A1331945"}}],"total":1},"links":{"self":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2FPhysRevLett.112.202502&size=5&page=1","bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2FPhysRevLett.112.202502&size=5&page=1&format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2FPhysRevLett.112.202502&size=5&page=1&format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2FPhysRevLett.112.202502&size=5&page=1&format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2FPhysRevLett.112.202502&size=5&page=1&format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2FPhysRevLett.112.202502&size=5&page=1&format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2FPhysRevLett.112.202502&size=5&page=1&format=cv"}}